A||||9||Haze_Performance||Dash Map Pocket Removal?||||1||
Z||000000||Haze_Performance||04-20-2000||05:42 PM||||How do you remove a dash pocket? Do you just pull it out? Right now I have the little cloth strip. I would like to put in a pocket. Also anyone have an all black one to sell? or didnt they come in all black? If not how about a gray one?<P>Thanks<BR> <p>[This message has been edited by Haze_Performance (edited 04-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||04-20-2000||07:48 PM||||It is bolted on. It is held by 4 studs, with tin nuts inside the dash. Taking out the speaker is required at the very least to remove one without wrecking it. Even with the speaker out the thing won't be pleasant to work on. (Can we shoot the fool that came up with the side loading pocket? What the hell is that thing suposed to hold? Everything falls out of it.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Haze_Performance||04-21-2000||06:17 AM||||But after I remove the stock one, the pocket will fit right it the old ones place right?||||reg||5
Z||000003||theogre||04-21-2000||02:28 PM||||I don't know what you are trying to insall but that whole recessed pannel/pocket thing is one part. There is one stud in each corner of the pannel and the whole thing is siting in a recess in the main dash. It's a ---- to get off without removing the whole dash.<P>There are 2 stock panels for that area that I've seen. The one with the slagging side opening pocket and another with a top opening pocket that goes all the way across the recess. Both are mounted the same. the whole pannel and pocket come off together. You can't take the pocket off the pannel without wrecking both. (I'm changing mine from the side opener to the top open one. It's pocket is not very large but at least I can set something in it and it will stay.) The top opener looks much nicer. Even has the Fiero logo on it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mbramble||05-20-2000||11:55 AM||||I just finished replacing my original side-opening flap with a top opening type.  Looks much better and is infinitely more useful.<P>You must remove the dash.  The only nut that is accessible w/o doing so is the one you can touch with the speaker removed.  It would be very difficult to get that one out thru the speaker opening anyway.  The others are mounted behind the air duct -- actually between the back of the dash and the air duct which is screwed to the back of the dash.  Once the dash is removed, you must remove the duct to get to the other 3 nuts!!  Not terribly difficult, didn't take over an hour.  'Course I have gotten to be an absolute pro at taking off the forward sections of the center console!!  Be very careful inside the guage housing as many of the wire hold-downs in mine were very brittle and broke.  <P>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Guinea Pig||05-20-2000||06:52 PM||||If I recall correctly, when I put in an after market glove box the instructions said to grab the pocket back( the part that is against the dash) and pull hard and sharply one side at a time  The plastic rivet, one on each side, will snap and the whole unit will come off.  I did and it did.  There was no damage to the dash.  This was on an '87 GT. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||mbramble||05-20-2000||07:37 PM||||That may have to do with the way the aftermarket glovebox is mounted.  But, if you are installing a 'top-opening' pocket, they mount the same way as the side-opening ones; as Ogre mentions above -- 4 threaded studs held in with nuts on the back.  If you manage to remove the old one by just pulling, you still have to get behind the dash to tighten down the new one.  <P>Must be quite a difference in the mounting methods for the after market goveboxes and the factory map pockets - either style.  It would make sense that an after market supplier would come up with an easier way of mounting their product than having to pull the dash!  Too bad Pontiac didn't....<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||87GTJohn||05-21-2000||12:30 PM||||Back when IRM was in full swing I bought a glove box for my 87 GT.  Instructions were to pull on the back part of the map pocket.  This broke the plastic pegs and the glove box was held on with two self tapping screws (big ones).  When I sold the car I kept the glove box and put the map pocket back in with 3M double sided tape, worked good.<BR>87GTJohn ||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||05-21-2000||02:35 PM||||without removing the dash, breaking the pegs off the pocket is the only way to remove it. I recently had my dash out. The back side of the pegs is only accessable once the air duct is removed. The pegs are anchored by some of those self threading tin nuts. Even if you could get a thin open end wrench on them I don't think you have room to turn it. the aire duct is about as tight to the back of the dash as they could get it.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Bill Osmer||05-22-2000||11:06 AM||||Haze -<P>I've just "upgraded" to a glove box - found one in a salvage yard. Anyway, I removed mine by the "yank & break" method. The glove box went in with self-tapping screws. <P>The available GT dash pocket is in very good condition, and I would recommend either the 3M tape method or "liquid Nails" or epoxy as an adhesive. There's a lot of surface area for good bonding, and I wouldn't have any problem suggesting bonding instead of threaded studs.<P>Email me if you want to do business.||||reg||7
